Follow the colour that goes bad, and correct what I concluded from the extra retain.

An instrument for #227 that took FOUR corrections to become trustworthy, and each wrong version
produced a confident, wrong reading. Recording them because the failures are the lesson:

  1. No unregistration at all. Fired 36 times on the ORDINARY case, a catalog colour and the
     colour it resolved dying together in one pool drain, which is correct behaviour.
  2. Unregister in catalog dealloc BEFORE releasing. That blinded it to the one case it exists
     for: the last holder frees the colour, and by then the pointer was out of the ring. Replaced
     with an expected-release window, so the paired free is ignored and anything else is reported.
  3. A 128 slot ring. Qt builds palettes from more catalog colours than that, so a pointer was
     silently EVICTED and reported "not registered, class unknown". A silent hook then looked
     like proof that no unexpected free happens, when it only meant the hook had lost track. Now
     8192 slots, and it prints how many it evicted so silence can be trusted.
  4. It never said what the object WAS when stored, only what reused its memory.

WHAT THE WORKING INSTRUMENT SAYS, on a death:

  BAD ENTRY self=0x... self-class=NSColor_catalog
  BAD ENTRY still-registered=no was-class=(unknown) evicted=0 of 46
  BAD ENTRY name=controlTextColor catalog=System entry=0x... class=__NSCFString

So the receiver is a LIVE, correctly classed NSColor_catalog whose _colorName still reads right,
nothing was evicted, and no unexpected free was ever reported. And yet _color holds a pointer
that WAS NEVER REGISTERED, while _color is assigned in exactly one place, the line beside the
registration.

THAT CANNOT BE A REFERENCE COUNT BUG, and it corrects what I wrote in d85b26da. The extra retain
taking 5 in 16 to 0 in 16 is consistent with an over-release, but it is equally consistent with
heap corruption, because any change in allocation size or order moves an out of bounds write
somewhere else. The new facts favour corruption: a live object whose field holds a pointer that
was never written there is a field that somebody else wrote.

It also explains why EVERY perturbation makes the bug vanish, which an over-release would not:
colour trace 0 of 8, zombies 0 of 8, extra retain 0 of 16.

NEXT is to find the writer, not the releaser. NSColor_catalog declares exactly one ivar, _color,
so an overrun into it comes from whatever is allocated beside it or from a write through a stale
pointer of the right shape.

--- a/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor_catalog.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor_catalog.m
@@ -34,6 +34,57 @@
 NSColor *NSColorGetCatalogColor(NSColorListName catalogName,
                                 NSColorName colorName);
 
+/*
+ * WHICH RESOLVED COLOURS A LIVE CATALOG ENTRY IS HOLDING, so that NSColor_CGColor dealloc can say
+ * when one of them is freed underneath us (#227). A fixed ring rather than a collection: this runs
+ * inside colour construction and must not allocate, and only the most recent entries can be the
+ * one about to die. Diagnostic, off unless CIDER_TRACE_COLORLIFE is set.
+ */
+/* BIG ENOUGH NOT TO EVICT. 128 was not: Qt builds its palettes from far more catalog colours than
+ * that, the ring wrapped, and a pointer that had been evicted reported "not registered" and
+ * "class unknown" at the failure. That made a silent hook look like proof no unexpected free had
+ * happened, when it only meant the hook had lost track. gCiderColorLifeWrapped says so out loud. */
+#define CIDER_COLORLIFE_SLOTS 8192
+static id gCiderColorLife[CIDER_COLORLIFE_SLOTS];
+static NSColorName gCiderColorLifeName[CIDER_COLORLIFE_SLOTS];
+/* The class it WAS when stored. The class at the crash is the reuser, which says nothing about
+ * what was freed, and printing it at store time would be a trace per lookup, which suppresses
+ * the bug. Recorded here and printed only from the failure path. */
+static const char *gCiderColorLifeClass[CIDER_COLORLIFE_SLOTS];
+static unsigned gCiderColorLifeNext;
+static unsigned gCiderColorLifeWrapped;
+
+/* Nonzero while a catalog colour is releasing the colour it owns, so that free is not reported. */
+unsigned _CiderExpectedColorRelease;
+
+void _CiderForgetHeldColor(id color) {
+    if (color == nil)
+        return;
+    for (unsigned i = 0; i < CIDER_COLORLIFE_SLOTS; i++)
+        if (gCiderColorLife[i] == color) {
+            gCiderColorLife[i] = nil;
+            gCiderColorLifeName[i] = nil;
+        }
+}
+
+const char *_CiderCatalogNameHoldingColor(id color) {
+    if (color == nil)
+        return NULL;
+    for (unsigned i = 0; i < CIDER_COLORLIFE_SLOTS; i++)
+        if (gCiderColorLife[i] == color)
+            return gCiderColorLifeName[i] != nil ? [gCiderColorLifeName[i] UTF8String] : "(unnamed)";
+    return NULL;
+}
+
+const char *_CiderCatalogClassHeldAs(id color) {
+    if (color == nil)
+        return NULL;
+    for (unsigned i = 0; i < CIDER_COLORLIFE_SLOTS; i++)
+        if (gCiderColorLife[i] == color)
+            return gCiderColorLifeClass[i];
+    return NULL;
+}
+
